# Coding Standards
## 1. Use Assertions
All my repos should have a function like `logErrorAndSendMSTeamsMessage`.
Things that should never happen should use this as an assertion as follows.
```
if (condition) {
  logErrorAndSendMSTeamsMessage(log, `condition <describe condition here> failed`);
  // handle error correctly here, by returning early, throwing an exception, returning 500, whatever is appropriate.
}
```
## 2. Error-Free/Warning-Free build/compile/linting/static analysis
All of my repos use `just b` to do a complete build, static analysis, and unit test.
This should run successfully without any warnings or errors after all changes.
## 3. Unit Test Pure Functions
When you can write pure functions that don't do any IO (no disk, no network, no
display to screen), favour writing those, and add comprehensive unit tests for
them. These unit tests should never require any mocks.
## 4. Integration Test Functions that do IO
If there is a pattern for integration testing already, follow that pattern and
add integration tests for functions that do IO. This should require no mocks
most of the time, and one or two mocks in very rare conditions.
If there isn't a pattern for integration testing already in the repo, then the
user hasn't made a decision about that yet, and you should not add integration tests.
## 5. Log
My repos should all have functions like `logInfo` and `logError`. Use them
everywhere that a branch is taken.
```
if (value.length === 0) {
  logInfo(log, `Value had no elements in the array`);
  return false;
}
logInfo(log, `Value had ${value.length} elements`);
// continue processing value here
```
Be sure not to log anything that could be PII or PHI.
## 6. Record all bugs you see
If at any point in writing code/running code you see a bug, even if it's not related to
what you're doing, run `uuidgen`, and make a note of it in a file called
`bugs_<uuid>.md` in the repo root. Then, continue with your task.
## 7. Check return values of all non-void functions
If it's a value that requires error handling, handle the error. If it's a value
that should always be something expected, assert on it. Either way, log something
about the result.
## 8. War against complexity
If a change could be a hundred lines, and you've changed 250, stop and re-assess.
If a decision made in the past was bad and is making things hard, stop, run `uuidgen`, write a
file called `foundation_fix_required_<uuid>.md` and put the details in there.
If you're just choosing a more complicated solution for no benefit, stop and
implement the simpler solution instead.
If the more complicated solution is better, run `uuidgen`, and add a file called
`complexity_justification_<uuid>.md` and explain your decision.
